<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Communicating Panama's Importance – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> International Communication</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Communicating Panama's Importance</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Reading: Comprehend complex narrative texts. Reading: Analyze persuasive pieces on current events.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can comprehend narratives that introduce Panama's global role, identifying main events (e.g., "In the story, Panama's strategic position in global trade is highlighted, showing how the Panama Canal plays a crucial role in connecting oceans and facilitating international commerce.")
Can analyze persuasive elements in articles promoting Panama (e.g., "The article uses phrases like 'Panama is a key player in global trade' and 'a vital hub for international commerce' to emphasize Panama's global importance.")
Can discuss themes of cultural pride in narratives about Panama (e.g., "The text promotes values of sustainability and conservation, celebrating Panama's biodiversity as a source of national pride and ecological importance.")</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activating prior knowledge and introducing the topic of the lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a world map and points to Panama. Students are asked to brainstorm, in pairs, "WHAT MAKES PANama a KEY PLAYer in GLOBAL trade and why is its BIODIVERSITY so VALUABLE globally?" (Stress on Wh- Question word and target vocabulary). Students share their ideas with the class, recalling vocabulary from the previous lesson such as 'trade', 'hub', 'biodiversity', 'strategic'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models reading sentences related to Panama's importance, emphasizing the pronunciation of /t/ in "trade" and /g/ in "global". For example, "Panama is recognized for its strategic position in global trade." The teacher also models stressing modal verbs, saying: "You MUST be proficient in ENGLISH to effectively COMMUNICATE Panama's global relevance."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'biodiversity' mean in the context of Panama's natural resources?" (Target: 'biodiversity', 'natural') – Expected answer: "It refers to the diverse plant and animal life found in Panama's protected areas."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a country is a 'trade hub', what does that imply about its role in international commerce?" (Target: 'trade hub', 'international commerce') – Expected answer: "It means it's a central point where goods and financial services move through for global shipping."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to the Scenario: "International Communication".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students are provided with an authentic narrative text titled "Panama: A Global Hub That Connects Continents", an excerpt from an online article. The text's register is formal and informative, suitable for a professional setting. It incorporates key vocabulary such as 'trade', 'relevance', 'biodiversity', 'financial services', 'hub', 'strategic', and includes examples of passive voice ("Panama is recognized for...", "Challenges are faced..."), modals of necessity ("Panama must ensure...", "We should consider..."), and relative clauses ("...a trade hub that connects continents").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The ability to comprehend and analyze complex informative and persuasive texts about global issues, extracting main ideas and specific details relevant to international communication. (Specific for Reading skills at B1.3).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the article individually.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the text quickly to identify the main topic of each paragraph and write a short phrase (2-3 words) next to each paragraph summarizing its content.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Questions:</b> Students answer 5 True/False questions based on specific details mentioned in the text. Example: "Panama's biodiversity is considered irrelevant to its global standing." (False)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match key phrases from the article with their corresponding explanations or examples. Example: Match "critical for global shipping" with "Panama Canal's significant impact".</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading. Reinforcing target grammar and vocabulary through structured activities.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension for specific grammar (passive voice, modals of necessity, relative clauses) and vocabulary ('trade', 'relevance', 'biodiversity', 'financial services', 'hub', 'strategic', 'impact', 'challenges').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Grammar Spotting:</b> Students reread the article and underline all instances of passive voice (e.g., "Panama is recognized for...") in blue, all modal verbs of necessity (e.g., "must ensure...") in green, and all relative clauses (e.g., "that connects continents") in red. They then write down three sentences of each type in their notebooks, identifying the main subject or action.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Vocabulary Cloze:</b> Students are given a short summary of the article with 8-10 blanks. They must fill in the blanks using a provided word bank containing the target vocabulary from the article (e.g., 'relevance', 'biodiversity', 'professionals', 'commerce', 'hub', 'transit', 'ecological', 'strategic'). This reinforces reading for contextual vocabulary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – Persuasive Elements Analysis:</b> In pairs, students read specific paragraphs identified by the teacher that contain persuasive language (e.g., "Panama is a vital hub...", "Its ecological significance cannot be overstated."). They discuss how these phrases aim to convince the reader of Panama's importance. The teacher encourages them to use phrases like: "From my perspective, this phrase emphasizes...", "The article uses 'key player' to make us consider...".</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "International Communication".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a different short informational text (e.g., a "fact sheet" or "press release excerpt") detailing a specific aspect of Panama's global importance: one on the Panama Canal's impact on global trade, another on Panama's biodiversity and conservation efforts, and a third on its financial services sector.
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Panama's Global Briefing" - Each group reads their assigned text thoroughly, identifying main ideas, supporting details, persuasive language, and key vocabulary. They then collaboratively prepare a short summary (maximum 3 minutes) of their text's content to "brief" a simulated international delegation (the rest of the class). The focus is on comprehending, synthesizing, and then presenting information concisely, using the target grammar and vocabulary from their reading. They must ensure all viewpoints from their text are acknowledged and effectively communicated to "lead presentations on Panama's global role".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This task encourages reading for specific information, analysis of persuasive elements, and then the use of modals (e.g., "We must recognize...", "Panama should maintain...") and passive voice (e.g., "The Canal is operated...", "Many challenges are faced...") while communicating their understanding.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy to gauge student comprehension and application.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during "Panama's Global Briefing" and peer assessment of each group's summary and presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student will submit a short written paragraph (5-7 sentences) summarizing the main points of their group's assigned reading text and identifying one example of a persuasive phrase used. The teacher will also observe group discussions and presentations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Comprehension: Accuracy of main events and details identified from the reading text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Analysis: Ability to identify and explain a persuasive element in the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Target Language Use: Correct use of at least two vocabulary words from the list ('hub', 'strategic', 'biodiversity', 'commerce', 'impact') and one grammatical structure (passive voice, modal, relative clause) in the summary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity & Cohesion: Overall coherence and intelligibility of the written summary.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most significant point you learned about Panama's global role from today's reading?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which vocabulary word or grammar structure from the lesson do you feel you used most effectively today?"</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Reviewing the lesson's effectiveness and planning for future instruction.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did analyzing the persuasive language in the texts help you understand Panama's importance more deeply?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What challenges did you face when reading the complex narrative texts, and how might you overcome them next time?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The students showed good comprehension of the main ideas and were able to identify some persuasive elements. The group briefing activity effectively combined reading for synthesis with communicative output. Some students struggled with the nuanced usage of relative clauses and passive voice in their summaries, requiring more targeted practice. Scaffolding for the next lesson will include reviewing these structures and providing sentence frames for spoken output to build confidence. The pronunciation practice of /t/ and /g/ needs to be integrated more explicitly into reading aloud activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This reading lesson provided the content and linguistic structures. Lesson 3 will focus on Speaking & Interaction, where students will actively use the target vocabulary and grammar (passive voice, modals, relative clauses) in paired and group discussions to communicate Panama's importance based on the information they read and analyzed today, preparing them to "engage in conversations about Panama's role in global trade."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>